[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/LeninPerez"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/3p4/3PP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2021.02.03"]
[UTCTime "17:32:06"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ You already know how to play against e5 (Advance Variation) and exd5 (Exchange Variation), all of these, pawn movements.

Now I will show you how to play against the movements of the g1 knight (Nc3 and Nd2) :D } { [%csl Bd2,Bc3][%cal Ye4e5,Ye4d5,Bb1c3,Bb1d2] }
1. Nc3 { Let's start with Nc3. This is called the Classical Variation. The white player begins to develop his pieces.
Capture the pawn } { [%cal Bb1c3] } 1... dxe4 2. Nxe4 { Now I will give you 3 options to continue.
What would you play in this position?
- Bf5
- Nf6
- Nd7 } { [%csl Yf6,Yf5,Yd7][%cal Yb8d7,Yg8f6,Yc8f5] } 2... Bf5 { The best move!
It is the best because this movement expels the knight from the central square e4 } 3. Ng3 { Retire your bishop } { [%csl Rf5] } 3... Bg6 4. Nf3 { Play Nd7 } { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 4... Nd7 5. Bd3 { The dark-squared bishop is very sad.
How would you bring this bishop to life? } { [%csl Rf8] } 5... e6 { Excellent! } 6. O-O { Well, the bishop has life, but it needs a better square. Can you find it? } { [%csl Gf8] } 6... Bd6 *


